---
import BlogLayout from "../../../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import ArticleImage from "../../../../components/ui/ArticleImage.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro";
---

<BlogLayout
  title="Conventional Loan vs DSCR Loan for Investment Property: Which One Fits | Cliffco Mortgage"
  description="Choosing between a conventional loan and a DSCR loan for investment property goes beyond the rate. The right answer depends on how your income looks on paper and how the property cash flows."
  type="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/" },
    { name: "Conventional Loan vs DSCR Loan", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/conventional-loan-vs-dscr-loan-investment-property/" },
  ]} />

  <article class="container-prose pt-12 pb-16">

    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Conventional Loan vs DSCR Loan for Investment Property: How to Pick the Right One Before You Apply</h1>

    <ArticleImage />

    <p class="mt-8 font-bold">If You're Stuck Choosing Between Two Loan Types</p>

    <p class="mt-4">If you're weighing a conventional loan against a DSCR loan for your next rental property and you're not sure which one actually fits your situation, you're asking the right question at the right time. Applying for the wrong product doesn't just slow you down. It costs you credit inquiries, time, and sometimes the deal itself.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Comparison Most Investors Start in the Wrong Place</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Most comparisons between conventional and DSCR loans focus on rate. That's the wrong starting point. The real question is qualification, not pricing. A conventional loan at a lower rate that you can't qualify for isn't better than a DSCR loan at a higher rate that closes on time. The rate comparison only matters after you've confirmed which product you actually fit into. Start there, not at the rate sheet.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Each Loan Is Built Around a Different Version of You</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Conventional loans are built around the borrower. Your documented income, your debt-to-income ratio, your employment history, your tax returns. If you look strong on paper, conventional pricing rewards you for it. If your income is complex, reduced by write-offs, or inconsistent year over year, conventional underwriting works against you regardless of how strong the property is.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">DSCR loans are built around the property. The lender doesn't care what you made last year. They care whether the rent covers the mortgage at a sufficient ratio. If the property performs and your credit is solid, DSCR gets you into deals that conventional would deny, sometimes on the same property, with the same borrower, evaluated in the same week.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The problem is that most investors don't know which version of themselves the lender is actually evaluating until they're already in underwriting.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How Conventional and DSCR Loans Compare on the Factors That Matter</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The two loan types diverge on four dimensions that determine whether a deal works.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Income documentation.</strong> Conventional requires full documentation of personal income, two years of tax returns, and a debt-to-income calculation that includes all your existing obligations. DSCR requires none of that. The property's rent is the income.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Rate.</strong> Conventional rates are lower. DSCR rates carry a premium to reflect the reduced documentation and higher lender risk. That gap matters over the life of the loan, but it's not the deciding factor for most investors choosing between products.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Coverage ratio.</strong> DSCR loans require the property to produce enough rent to cover the mortgage payment at a ratio most lenders set at 1.10 or higher. A property with strong cash flow qualifies easily. A property with thin margins may not qualify for DSCR at all, because the higher DSCR rate eats into the coverage ratio. This is the math most people miss. DSCR is not a universal alternative to conventional. The property has to perform at a sufficient level to absorb the rate premium.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Entity ownership.</strong> DSCR loans are generally more flexible about closing in an LLC. Conventional loans issued to individuals can create complications when later transferred to an entity. If your portfolio strategy involves entity ownership, that alone can tip the decision toward DSCR.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Calculate the property's DSCR at current DSCR market rates before you decide anything.</strong> Divide monthly gross rent by estimated total monthly payment. If it's below 1.10, standard DSCR pricing likely won't work. Know this before you apply.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculate your conventional debt-to-income ratio using your actual documented income.</strong> Determine the maximum total monthly debt your lender will allow and check whether the new payment plus your existing obligations fits under that ceiling.</li>
      <li><strong>Check whether your portfolio strategy involves LLC ownership.</strong> If yes, lean toward DSCR from the start rather than restructuring later.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare total cost over your expected hold period, not just the rate.</strong> A rate premium on a DSCR loan spread over a short hold period may cost less than a delayed closing or a denied conventional application.</li>
      <li><strong>Apply once, to the right product.</strong> Every hard credit pull has an impact. Do the analysis before anyone pulls your credit.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Run Both Scenarios Before You Commit to Either</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Use Cliffco's Payment Estimator below to model the monthly payment under both conventional and DSCR rates on the same property, then compare the coverage ratio each scenario produces. If you're self-employed and need to evaluate your conventional qualifying income first, the <a href="/mortgage-guides/calculators/self-employed-income/">Self-Employed Income Calculator</a> will show you that number before you ever sit across from an underwriter.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Pick the product that fits the deal. Then work on the rate.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Model the Payment on Your Investment Property</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Use the Payment Estimator below to see your projected monthly principal and interest at different purchase prices and rates. That baseline makes it easier to compare what you'd qualify for on a DSCR program versus a conventional loan.</p>

    <!-- Embedded lite Payment Estimator -->
    <div class="mt-12 border border-[var(--color-accent)]/25 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-[var(--color-accent)]/8 px-6 py-5 border-b border-[var(--color-accent)]/15">
        <p class="eyebrow text-xs mb-1">Quick check</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Estimate your monthly payment.</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Enter the numbers for any property to see your estimated monthly payment before you make an offer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6">
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Home price ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-price" type="number" value="500000" min="50000" step="5000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Down payment (%)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-down" type="number" value="20" min="3" max="50" step="1" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Interest rate (%)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-rate" type="number" value="7.0" min="1" max="15" step="0.125" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Loan term</label>
            <select id="art-pe-term" class="art-calc-input">
              <option value="30">30-year fixed</option>
              <option value="20">20-year fixed</option>
              <option value="15">15-year fixed</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Annual taxes ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-taxes" type="number" value="6000" min="0" step="100" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Annual insurance ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-ins" type="number" value="2400" min="0" step="100" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <button id="art-pe-btn" class="btn btn-primary mt-5">Estimate Payment</button>

        <div id="art-pe-result" class="hidden mt-6">
          <div class="p-5 bg-[var(--color-mist-gray)] rounded-lg">
            <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Principal &amp; Interest</p>
                <p id="art-pe-pi" class="text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Taxes &amp; Insurance</p>
                <p id="art-pe-ti" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Total Monthly Payment</p>
                <p id="art-pe-total" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p id="art-pe-note" class="mt-3 text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]"></p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-4 p-5 bg-[var(--color-rich-black)] rounded-lg flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4 justify-between">
            <div>
              <p class="text-white font-semibold">Know your number before you make an offer.</p>
              <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mt-1">A Cliffco loan officer can lock a rate and get you pre-approved the same day.</p>
            </div>
            <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary !bg-white !text-[var(--color-rich-black)] shrink-0 whitespace-nowrap">Talk to a loan officer</a>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-center text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
            Want to model other scenarios? <a href="/mortgage-guides/calculators/payment-estimator/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] font-medium">View the full payment estimator →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </article>
</BlogLayout>

<style>
  .art-calc-input {
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-soft-gray, #d1d5db);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: white;
    outline: none;
    transition: border-color 0.15s;
  }
  .art-calc-input:focus {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 15%, transparent);
  }
</style>

<script>
  const peBtn = document.getElementById("art-pe-btn")!;
  peBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const price = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-price") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const downPct = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-down") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const rate = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-rate") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const term = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-term") as HTMLSelectElement).value;
    const taxes = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-taxes") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const ins = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-ins") as HTMLInputElement).value;

    const loanAmount = price * (1 - downPct / 100);
    const r = rate / 100 / 12;
    const n = term * 12;
    const pi = r === 0 ? loanAmount / n : loanAmount * (r * Math.pow(1 + r, n)) / (Math.pow(1 + r, n) - 1);
    const ti = (taxes + ins) / 12;
    const total = pi + ti;
    const fmt = (v: number) => "$" + Math.round(v).toLocaleString("en-US");

    (document.getElementById("art-pe-pi") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(pi);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-ti") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(ti);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-total") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(total);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-note") as HTMLElement).textContent =
      `Loan amount: ${fmt(loanAmount)} | Down payment: ${fmt(price * downPct / 100)}. Estimate only. Does not include PMI, HOA, or other costs.`;
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-result") as HTMLElement).classList.remove("hidden");
  });
</script>
